the war in Syria made Abdullah Ayyoub  and his family refugees in their country  he also lost his son it's been four  years since Abdul Rahman disappeared  while traveling to neighboring Lebanon  his taxi driver told the family he was  taken at a government checkpoint please  help me tell me where he is I just want  to hear his voice I don't know anything  about him I just want to know if he's  alive or dead there are many others in a  similar position human rights groups  have documented the detention abduction  and enforced disappearance of at least  130 thousand Syrians most had taken part  in peaceful protests when the uprising  began in 2011 calls by the UN and other  organizations for their release from  government jails have grown louder with  the spread of coronavirus in late March  Syrian President Bashar Assad issued an  amnesty for some crimes without  mentioning political prisoners rights  groups say the decree was a tactic to  avoid international pressure they have  documented the release of less than 100  people jailed for common crimes while  more than 100 were arrested in the same  period other merciful hamantash mahalia  it was the 18th amnesty issued and still  130,000 political prisoners are in jails  there have also been deaths due to  torture despite the amnesties the Assad  government denies holding prisoners of  conscience and torturing detainees to  death in secret security prisons but you  and investigators say tens of thousands  of people have disappeared and may no  longer be alive some who survived like  dr. mohamed abu Hallel are turning to an  ally of Bashar al-assad for help he  wrote a letter to Russian President  Vladimir Putin which was published in  the Russian media I urged him to  pressure Bashar Assad to release the  arbitrary detain people the most  vulnerable so far my email is empty they  didn't provide me any response so far  but at least I did my duty actually to  hold him accountable actually years of  campaigning have done little to pressure  the government to release the prisoners  or allow independent monitors into  detention centers if you believe  anything will change for Abdul Rahman's  mother there doesn't seem to be any end  to her pain said Hoda al Jazeera Beirut
 

